  "summary": "Samurai Shodown II is SNK's weapon-based fighting game, first released for Neo Geo in October 1994.",
  "body": [
    "Samurai Shodown II is SNK's weapon-based fighting game, first released for Neo Geo in October 1994. Haohmaru and most of the original cast return alongside new fighters as Mizuki Rashojin becomes the central threat. The large weapons make distance and the timing of a single opening important, with the expanded movement system providing more ways to manage that space. The sequel refines the original's dramatic exchanges while giving players additional answers to attacks, so waiting within striking range becomes a more active contest of observation and response.",
    "Characters can roll forward or backwards, duck under high attacks and make small hops over low ones. A defensive command performed at the right moment can deflect an incoming strike and briefly expose the opponent. These options give particular attacks particular answers, encouraging the player to recognise their height and timing. A large swing may still promise substantial damage, but a prepared rival has another way to escape it or turn it aside. The encounter becomes a sequence of commitments, with each successful defensive movement creating a possible reply.",
    "The POW meter now supports powerful special techniques that can break an opponent's weapon. Fighting without that weapon changes the immediate balance until a replacement arrives, making the successful attack a disruption as well as a loss of health. Character-specific moves, hidden encounters and small appearances by other SNK figures add further reasons to explore the cast. The sequel's improvements serve its central spectacle: two armed fighters can produce a decisive exchange in a moment, and the expanded systems give both players more influence over how that moment is reached."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Another meeting of blades",
    "A movement that saves the round",
    "The danger of losing a weapon"
  ],
